# Derby Bentley 3½ / 4¼ litre closed saloon, 1933-1939.
#
# The "Silent Sports Car": Rolls-Royce's sporting Bentley, built at Derby from
# September 1933 on a shortened 20/25 chassis. 3,669 cc pushrod six with twin
# carburettors and a crossflow head, about 110-120 bhp; the 4,255 cc 4¼ litre
# replaced it in 1936. 1,177 of the 3½ were built, and the 4¼ passed that total
# by 1940. Coachbuilt saloons were supplied by Park Ward, Thrupp & Maberly,
# Hooper and others; the modelled form is the closed saloon.
# https://www.bentleymedia.com/en/heritage-collection/1934-3-and-a-half-litre-derby-axb3
# https://www.bdcl.org/club/bentley-history/derby-history
# http://www.simoncars.co.uk/bentley/derby35.html
#
# Dimensions: 4,419 mm long, about 1,680 mm wide, about 1,550 mm high, wheelbase
# 3,200 mm (126 in), track 1,422 mm.
#
# Colours: black was the usual finish; dark maroon, dark blue and cream were
# common coachbuilder choices. As with the Rolls-Royce 20/25 there was no factory
# body colour list, because the coachbuilder painted the body.
#
# Weight: about 2,400 cars over seven years, rarer on the road than the 20/25 and
# far rarer than any ordinary car; set at 4 in total, one per colour.
# Provisional pending the central rebase.
#
# Speed 120 km/h: a game-balance figure; period claims were 94-97 mph.
# Comfort 78/447: the same long-wheelbase, softly sprung coachbuilt standard as the
# 20/25, so it sits between the 20/25 (72) and the 25/30 (80); the line gives 447.
#
# Dates describe road presence: 1933 to about 1950.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/derby-bentley.blend in the blends repository.
# References: citycars/references/derby-bentley/ (gitignored).
# Image credits: 1937 4¼ saloon and 1934 3½ tourer photographs via Wikimedia
# Commons (see reference file names for the photographers and licences).

